   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Dear Cardinal MSI Help &amp; Kylie Bemis</div><div><br></div><div>I wish to inquire if its possible to select multiple region of interest and write/export/save them as data frames (with all the mass features and intensities) for further multivariate analysis.</div><div>Here is my code below.</div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:10pt!important;outline:none;border:none;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;white-space:pre-wrap!important;line-height:15px;color:rgb(255,255,255);font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(0,34,64)\"><span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,157,0)\">ROI_5 &lt;-  selectROI(data1_processed, formula = ~ x * y,\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,157,0);white-space:pre\">+ </span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,157,0)\">                    mode = c(&quot;region&quot;, &quot;pixels&quot;),\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,157,0);white-space:pre\">+ </span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,157,0)\">                    main = &quot;data1_processed&quot;, subset = TRUE, lattice = FALSE)\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,98,140)\">Select pixels and press ESC or second mouse button when done\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,157,0);white-space:pre\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,157,0)\">                    ROI_image = data1_processed [,ROI_5]\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,157,0);white-space:pre\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,157,0)\">                    image (ROI_image, mz=725.55)</span></span></pre><b></b><i></i><u></u><sub></sub><sup></sup><strike></strike><br></div><div><b></b><i></i><u></u><sub></sub><sup></sup><strike></strike><br></div><div>Kind regards,</div><div>Shadrack</div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "VmqD55COGwAJ",
   "from": "kbemis",
   "author": "6033d3c0e7d242fa",
   "date": "2019-01-23T19:27:05Z",
   "subject": "Re: Export ROI as Data Frame",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Shadrack,<div><br></div><div>Firstly, I&#39;ll note you don&#39;t really need most of those arguments to selectROI() besides the first one (and possibly &quot;main&quot;), as they&#39;re all the defaults anyway.</div><div><br></div><div>As for selecting multiple ROI, it depends what do you want to do with them? The ROI are simply returned as a logical vector, so you can manipulate them as you wish.</div><div><br></div><div>For example, if you selected two ROI and assigned them to variables &quot;roi1&quot; and &quot;roi2&quot;, you could do:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><font color=\"#660066\"><span style=\"color:#000\">myroi </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> roi1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">|</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> roi2</span></font></div></code></div><br>to combine them into a single larger ROI using R&#39;s vectorized &quot;OR&quot; operator.</div><div><br></div><div>You could also do:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">condition </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">NA_character_</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ncol</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mydata</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>condition</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">roi1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;Condition 1&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>condition</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">roi2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;Condition 2&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>condition </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">as</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">factor</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">condition</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><font color=\"#000000\"></font></div></code></div><br>to create a categorical variable (a factor) with two ROI&#39;s in it.</div><div><br></div><div>How do you want the data frame of intensities and masses formatted? Do you want, for example, each column to be a mass spectrum?</div><div><br></div><div>You could do this like:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><font><span style=\"color:#000\">myroi </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> selectROI</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mydata</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>myroi_spectra </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">as</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mydata</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)[,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">myroi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">])</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>write</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">csv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">myroi_spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><font><span style=\"color:#000\">file</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;spectra.csv&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>write</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">csv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">pData</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><font color=\"#000000\"><span><span style=\"color:#000\">mydata</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">myroi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,],</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> file</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;coord.csv&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></span></font><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>write</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">csv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fData</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><font color=\"#000000\"><span><span style=\"color:#000\">mydata</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> file</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;mz.csv&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></span></font></font></font></div></code></div><br>In this case, each column of &quot;spectra.csv&quot; is a mass spectrum, and the pixel coordinates and m/z values are stored in separate CSV files.</div><div><br></div><div>Does this accomplish what you want?</div><div><br></div><div>-Kylie</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
